   "id": "nar_campaign",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "November 1943. Tarawa Atoll, Gilbert Islands. The Second Marine Division will storm Betio, a fortified island barely three kilometres long, held by nearly five thousand Japanese troops. Admiral Shibasaki boasts that a million men could not take it in a hundred years."
  },
  {
   "id": "nar_s1",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "Day one. The tide is low. Amtracs will carry the first three waves over the reef. Higgins boats behind them may ground on the coral. Get your Marines to the seawall, and silence the guns on Red Beach."
  },
  {
   "id": "nar_s2",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "Day two. Major Ryan's scratch battalion clings to the western tip of Betio. Clear Green Beach of pillboxes, so the First Battalion, Sixth Marines can land."
  },
  {
   "id": "nar_s3",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "Day three. A massive sand-covered bombproof shelter blocks the advance east of Red Beach Three. Lieutenant Bonnyman's engineers must take it with flamethrowers and demolitions."
  },
  {
   "id": "nar_s4",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "Night of day three. The last Japanese defenders are gathering in the east of the island. Hold the line until dawn. Expect them to charge."
  },
  {
   "id": "nar_s5",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "November twenty seventh. Buariki, the far end of the atoll. The last Japanese garrison has retreated into the jungle. Hunt them down."
  },
  {
   "id": "nar_victory",
   "role": "narrator",
   "text": "Victory. The cost was terrible, but the lessons learned at Tarawa would save thousands of lives in the battles to come."
